import Doc from '../../layouts/Doc.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Doc
  title="RPN & NRPN"
  slug="rpn-nrpn"
  description="Registered and Non-Registered Parameter Numbers in MIDI 1.0: parameter selection, data entry, the RPN list, pitch bend range, and the null RPN."
  specs={[
    { label: 'MIDI 1.0 Core Specifications', href: 'https://midi.org/midi-1-0-core-specifications' },
    { label: 'All specs', href: 'https://midi.org/specs' },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    MIDI 1.0 has only 128 controller numbers. Parameter numbers get around that by using a few controllers to pick one of
    16,384 parameters and then setting its value with Data Entry. <strong>RPNs</strong> are parameters defined by the
    MIDI specification. <strong>NRPNs</strong> are left to each manufacturer.
  </p>
  <Callout title="Build the bytes">
    <p>
      The <a href="/tools/rpn/">RPN / NRPN builder</a> turns a parameter and a value in friendly units into the exact Control Change sequence, and decodes pasted streams.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="controllers">The controllers involved</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th class="num">CC</th><th>Hex</th><th>Function</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="num">101</td><td><code>0x65</code></td><td>RPN MSB (parameter number, high 7 bits)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">100</td><td><code>0x64</code></td><td>RPN LSB (parameter number, low 7 bits)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">99</td><td><code>0x63</code></td><td>NRPN MSB</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">98</td><td><code>0x62</code></td><td>NRPN LSB</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">6</td><td><code>0x06</code></td><td>Data Entry MSB (value, high 7 bits)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">38</td><td><code>0x26</code></td><td>Data Entry LSB (value, low 7 bits)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">96</td><td><code>0x60</code></td><td>Data Increment (+1; value byte ignored)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">97</td><td><code>0x61</code></td><td>Data Decrement (−1; value byte ignored)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sequence">The message sequence</h2>
  <p>A parameter change is a short series of Control Change messages on one channel:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Select the parameter.</strong> Send the parameter number MSB (CC 101 or 99), then the LSB (CC 100 or 98).
    </li>
    <li><strong>Set the value.</strong> Send Data Entry MSB (CC 6), then, if needed, Data Entry LSB (CC 38).</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deselect.</strong> Send the <a href="#null-rpn">null RPN</a> (CC 101 = 127, CC 100 = 127). Later Data Entry
      messages then change nothing by accident.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Once a parameter is selected it stays selected. Further Data Entry, Increment, or Decrement messages keep changing it
    until another parameter is chosen. Selecting an RPN deselects any NRPN, and the reverse, because both share Data
    Entry.
  </p>
  <Callout type="warn" title="Order matters">
    <p>
      Send the parameter number <strong>MSB before LSB</strong> and the data <strong>MSB before LSB</strong>. A Data Entry
      MSB resets the stored LSB on many receivers, so a lone LSB sent first is lost. Never interleave messages for two parameters on the
      same channel. Running status is fine.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="rpn-list">Registered Parameter Numbers</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>MSB, LSB</th><th>Parameter</th><th>Data Entry meaning</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr id="pitch-bend-sensitivity">
          <td><code>00, 00</code></td>
          <td>Pitch Bend Sensitivity</td>
          <td>MSB = semitones, LSB = cents. Default 2 semitones, 0 cents.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>00, 01</code></td>
          <td>Channel Fine Tuning</td>
          <td>14-bit, center 8192 (<code>40 00</code>) = A440. Range −100 cents to just under +100 cents, in steps of 100/8192 cent.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>00, 02</code></td>
          <td>Channel Coarse Tuning</td>
          <td>MSB only, center 64 = 0. Range −64 to +63 semitones. LSB ignored.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>00, 03</code></td>
          <td>Tuning Program Change</td>
          <td>MSB selects a MIDI Tuning Standard program (0–127).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>00, 04</code></td>
          <td>Tuning Bank Select</td>
          <td>MSB selects a MIDI Tuning Standard bank (0–127).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>00, 05</code></td>
          <td>Modulation Depth Range</td>
          <td>MSB = semitones, LSB = 128ths of a semitone (≈ 0.78 cents) of vibrato for full modulation (GM2).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>00, 06</code></td>
          <td>MPE Configuration Message</td>
          <td>Sent on the manager channel (1 or 16). MSB = number of member channels (0 disables the zone). See <a href="/docs/mpe/">MPE</a>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>3D, 00</code>–<code>3D, 08</code></td>
          <td>Three-Dimensional Sound Controllers</td>
          <td>Azimuth, elevation, gain, distance, and related parameters for positioning sound in 3D (RP-049).</td>
        </tr>
        <tr id="null-rpn">
          <td><code>7F, 7F</code></td>
          <td>Null Function (RPN Null)</td>
          <td>Deselects the current RPN/NRPN. Data Entry is ignored until a new parameter is chosen.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every other RPN number is reserved for future definition. NRPN numbers are left entirely to manufacturers. Check the
    instrument's MIDI implementation documentation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="example">Example: set pitch bend range to ±12 semitones</h2>
  <p>On channel 1:</p>
  <pre><code>B0 65 00   ; CC 101 RPN MSB = 0
B0 64 00   ; CC 100 RPN LSB = 0   → Pitch Bend Sensitivity selected
B0 06 0C   ; CC 6   Data Entry MSB = 12 semitones
B0 26 00   ; CC 38  Data Entry LSB = 0 cents
B0 65 7F   ; CC 101 = 127
B0 64 7F   ; CC 100 = 127         → Null RPN, deselect</code></pre>
  <p>With running status the same change takes 13 bytes:</p>
  <pre><code>B0 65 00 64 00 06 0C 26 00 65 7F 64 7F</code></pre>

  <h2 id="nrpn-example">Example: an NRPN</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose a synth's manual says NRPN <code>MSB 0x01, LSB 0x08</code> is the vibrato rate. To set it to 80 on channel 3:
  </p>
  <pre><code>B2 63 01   ; CC 99 NRPN MSB = 1
B2 62 08   ; CC 98 NRPN LSB = 8
B2 06 50   ; CC 6  Data Entry MSB = 80
B2 65 7F   ; CC 101 = 127
B2 64 7F   ; CC 100 = 127  → Null</code></pre>
  <p>
    Some NRPNs use the full 14-bit value (CC 6 followed by CC 38). Others use only the MSB. The manufacturer's
    documentation says which.
  </p>

  <h2 id="increment-decrement">Data Increment and Decrement</h2>
  <p>
    CC 96 and CC 97 step the selected parameter up or down. The specification leaves the step size to the parameter. For
    Pitch Bend Sensitivity, many devices step by one cent (the LSB) and carry into semitones. Support varies, so prefer
    absolute Data Entry when you can.
  </p>

  <h2 id="midi-2">In MIDI 2.0</h2>
  <p>
    The MIDI 2.0 protocol drops the CC sequence. It has dedicated <em>Registered Controller</em> and <em>Assignable
    Controller</em> messages (the new names for RPN and NRPN) that carry the bank, index, and a 32-bit value in one atomic
    packet, plus <em>per-note</em> versions. See <a href="/docs/midi-2/">MIDI 2.0 &amp; UMP</a>.
  </p>
</Doc>
